from mk import *
import math
# ---------------- Faraday and Lenz
lab_f=lab('fa-canvas','A bar magnet moving through a coil, with a meter showing the induced EMF and graphs of the flux and EMF over time.',
  seg('fa-mode','Move the magnet',[('auto','It swings automatically'),('drag','I drag it')]),
  controls(('fa-m','Magnet strength (A·m²)',1,10,1,5),('fa-N','Turns in the coil',10,300,10,100),('fa-f','Swing frequency (Hz)',.2,2,.1,.8)),
  readout(('fa-phi','Flux through the coil Φ','ef'),('fa-dphi','Rate of change dΦ/dt',''),('fa-emf','Induced EMF ε = −N dΦ/dt','frc'),('fa-dir','Induced current seen from the right','')),
  'The magnet\'s north pole (red) is on its right. Flux is largest when the magnet is centered in the coil. EMF appears only while the flux is changing, and its sign flips when the magnet changes from approaching to leaving. Switch to "I drag it" and move the magnet yourself: faster motion makes a larger EMF.')
fa_svg=svg(f'<circle cx="130" cy="115" r="70" style="fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ca) 6%,transparent);stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:2"/>'+
  ''.join(txt(x,y,'×','bf',15) for x in (95,130,165) for y in (85,120,155))+
  f'<path d="M 130 52 A 63 63 0 1 1 129 52" style="fill:none;stroke:var(--cv);stroke-width:3"/>'+arr(88,72,100,62,'cv','',2.2)+txt(130,38,'N = 200, r = 4.0 cm','ink',12)+txt(130,218,'B into page, decreasing: Lenz → clockwise current','mut',10),
  'A coil in a field that points into the page and is decreasing. The induced current must make more field into the page, so it flows clockwise.')
module('faraday','Faraday &amp; Lenz',[('ap','AP C Unit 13'),('pu','AP 2: magnetism')],
 'Change the magnetic flux through a loop and an EMF appears in it. This one law drives generators, transformers, microphones and induction stoves, and Lenz\'s law tells you which way the current goes: always so as to fight the change.',
 ['Hold a loop of wire in a magnetic field: nothing happens. Move a magnet toward it, spin it, or change the field strength: the meter jumps. What matters is not the field itself but how the <b>flux</b> Φ = B A cos θ changes. Faraday\'s law says the induced EMF equals minus the rate of change of flux, ε = −dΦ/dt (times N for a coil of N turns).',
  'Three ways to change the flux: change B, change the area A, or change the angle θ. A bigger rate of change, or more turns, gives a bigger EMF. A steady flux, however large, gives no EMF at all.',
  'The minus sign is <b>Lenz\'s law</b>: the induced current flows in the direction that creates a magnetic field opposing the change in flux. Magnet\'s north pole pushing in? The coil makes a north face to push back. Nature resists: this is energy conservation, because you must do work to push the magnet in, and that work becomes electrical energy.'],
 [('Φ<sub>B</sub> = ∫ <i class="bf">B</i> · d<i>A</i> = <i class="bf">B</i><i>A</i> cos θ','magnetic flux (weber)'),('ε = −<i>N</i> dΦ<sub>B</sub>/d<i>t</i>','Faraday\'s law'),('<i class="cur">I</i> = ε/<i>R</i> and Lenz: oppose the change','direction of the induced current')],
 add3(lab_f,'fa3-canvas','The same magnet and coil in space. The green arrows around the coil show the induced current: they reverse when the magnet turns around, and vanish when the magnet stops.','3D magnet and coil with induced current. Drag to rotate.'),
 [('Let the magnet swing automatically. Watch the EMF as it passes through the coil.',[["click","#fa-mode [data-k=auto]"],["#fa-m",5],["#fa-N",100],["#fa-f",.8]],'The EMF is largest when the magnet is moving fastest through the strongly changing part of the field, and it is zero at the instant the flux is at its maximum (centered) and at the ends of the swing.'),
  ('Double the number of turns.',[["click","#fa-mode [data-k=auto]"],["#fa-N",200]],'Each turn gets the same EMF, and they add in series, so ε doubles. The flux per turn is unchanged.'),
  ('Double the swing frequency.',[["click","#fa-mode [data-k=auto]"],["#fa-f",1.6]],'The same flux change happens in half the time, so the EMF doubles (and the graph squeezes horizontally).'),
  ('Switch to dragging. Move the magnet in slowly, then quickly.',[["click","#fa-mode [data-k=drag]"]],'Slow motion gives a small EMF; fast motion gives a large one. Hold the magnet still inside the coil: the EMF is exactly zero, even though the flux is at its maximum.')],
 dict(prob='A flat coil of 200 turns and radius 4.0 cm sits with its plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field. The field decreases from 0.50 T to zero in 0.10 s. (a) Find the average induced EMF. (b) If the coil\'s resistance is 10 Ω, find the average current. (c) Which way does the current flow if B points into the page?',svg=fa_svg,cap='A coil in a field pointing into the page, decreasing in strength.',
  steps=[('Flux change.',m('<i>A</i> = π(0.040)² = 5.03 × 10⁻³ m²')+'. The field is perpendicular to the plane, so cos θ = 1: '+m('ΔΦ = Δ<i class="bf">B</i>·<i>A</i> = (0 − 0.50)(5.03 × 10⁻³) = −2.5 × 10⁻³ Wb')+' per turn.'),
   ('EMF.',m('ε = −<i>N</i>ΔΦ/Δ<i>t</i> = −(200)(−2.5 × 10⁻³)/0.10 = +5.0 V')+'. (The size is what matters here: 5.0 V.)'),
   ('Current.',m('<i class="cur">I</i> = ε/<i>R</i> = 5.0/10 = 0.50 A')+'.'),
   ('Direction (Lenz).','The flux into the page is decreasing. The coil opposes that by making its own field <em>into</em> the page inside it, which requires a <b>clockwise</b> current (right-hand rule: fingers curl clockwise, thumb into the page).'),
   ('Check.','Larger N, larger A, bigger field change or faster change would each increase ε. Nothing would happen at all if B were constant ✓.')]),
 ['Writing ε = −dB/dt. It is the flux that matters: B, A and the angle can all change.','Using the sign of ε to decide direction without checking with Lenz\'s law. Always ask: what is changing, and how can the coil oppose it?','Thinking a large steady field makes a large EMF. It only matters how fast the flux changes.','Forgetting the factor of N for a coil with many turns.'],
 '''        <h2>Faraday's law in practice</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Flux:</b> <span class="m">Φ = ∫ <i class="bf">B</i> · d<i>A</i></span>. For a flat loop in a uniform field, <span class="m">Φ = <i class="bf">B</i><i>A</i> cos θ</span>, where θ is the angle between B and the loop's normal.</li>
          <li><b>EMF:</b> <span class="m">ε = −<i>N</i> dΦ/d<i>t</i></span>. For a graph of Φ against time, the EMF is minus the <em>slope</em> times N.</li>
          <li><b>AC generator:</b> a coil rotating at angular speed ω in a field has Φ = <i>BA</i> cos ωt, so <span class="m">ε = <i>NBA</i>ω sin ωt</span>: a sine wave.</li>
          <li><b>Induced electric field:</b> a changing flux creates a swirling electric field, so <span class="m">∮ <i class="ef">E</i> · d<i>ℓ</i> = −dΦ<sub>B</sub>/d<i>t</i></span>. This field is not conservative: it has no potential.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Lenz's law</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Steps: (1) which way is the flux, and is it increasing or decreasing? (2) the induced field opposes the <em>change</em>: if the flux is increasing, it points the other way; if decreasing, the same way; (3) right-hand rule to get the current.</li>
          <li><b>Energy:</b> pushing a magnet into a closed loop is harder than pushing it into an open one. The extra work you do becomes heat in the loop.</li>
          <li><b>Eddy currents:</b> in a solid conductor (a metal plate) a changing flux makes swirling currents that resist the motion: used for brakes and induction cookers.</li>
        </ul>''',
 dict(correct=1,explain='An EMF is induced only when the flux changes. A magnet at rest inside the coil makes a constant flux, so dΦ/dt = 0 and there is no EMF, however strong the field is.',q='a bar magnet is held motionless inside a coil of wire. The induced EMF in the coil is:',opts=['Large, because the field is strong','Zero, because the flux is not changing','Small but nonzero','Positive for north poles and negative for south poles']))
# ---------------- Motional EMF
lab_m=lab('mo-canvas','A conducting rod sliding on rails in a magnetic field that points into the page, with graphs of speed and current.',
  '<div class="row"><button type="button" class="btn primary" id="mo-go">Launch</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="mo-rst">Reset</button></div>',
  controls(('mo-B','Field B (T)',.2,1,.05,.5),('mo-L','Rod length L (m)',.2,.6,.05,.4),('mo-R','Resistance R (Ω)',.2,2,.1,.5),('mo-mass','Mass m (kg)',.05,.5,.05,.2),('mo-F','Applied force F (N)',0,2,.1,0),('mo-v0','Launch speed v₀ (m/s)',0,5,.5,4)),
  readout(('mo-emf','EMF = BLv','frc'),('mo-I','Current I = EMF/R','cur'),('mo-Fm','Magnetic braking force BIL',''),('mo-P','Power heated in R = I²R',''),('mo-vT','Terminal speed FR/(B²L²)','')),
  'The rod is launched with speed v₀ and, if F is not zero, pulled with a constant force. The moving rod acts as a battery with EMF BLv. The current it drives feels a magnetic force that opposes the motion (Lenz\'s law). With F = 0 the rod coasts to a stop; with F > 0 it speeds up until the forces balance.')
mo_svg=svg(ln(60,60,230,60,'ink',3)+ln(60,170,230,170,'ink',3)+ln(60,60,60,80,'ink',3)+ln(60,150,60,170,'ink',3)+
  f'<polyline points="60,80 52,86 68,92 52,98 68,104 52,110 68,116 52,122 68,128 52,134 68,140 60,146 60,150" style="fill:none;stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:2"/>'+
  ln(180,55,180,175,'ink',6)+arr(180,115,225,115,'cur','v',2.6,(0,-10))+arr(160,150,160,82,'cur','I',2.2,(-10,0))+arr(180,80,150,80,'frc','F',2.2,(-8,-8))+
  ''.join(txt(x,y,'×','bf',14) for x in (95,125,155,205) for y in (90,125,160))+txt(40,119,'R','mut',12,'end')+txt(130,205,'L = 0.50 m','mut',11)+txt(150,32,'B into the page','bf',12),
  'The rod moves right. The loop\'s area increases, the flux into the page grows, so the induced current is counterclockwise (up through the rod). The magnetic force on it points left.')
module('motional','Motional EMF',[('ap','AP C Unit 13'),('pu','AP 2: magnetism')],
 'A conductor moving through a magnetic field makes a voltage: the field pushes the charges inside it sideways. Complete the circuit and a current flows, which feels a braking force. This is an electric generator in its simplest form, and a clear case where Faraday, Lenz and energy conservation all agree.',
 ['Slide a metal rod to the right through a magnetic field pointing into the page. The charges in the rod move with it, so the magnetic force qv × B pushes the positive charges toward one end and the negative toward the other. That separation creates an EMF between the ends: <b>ε = BLv</b>.',
  'Put the rod on rails with a resistor and you have a circuit: current I = BLv/R flows. But a current-carrying rod in a magnetic field feels a force F = ILB, and (Lenz\'s law) it opposes the motion. To keep the rod moving at constant speed you have to push with exactly that force, and the work you do equals the heat produced in the resistor: Fv = I²R.',
  'If you let go, the rod slows down exponentially with time constant τ = mR/(B²L²): faster means more EMF, more current, more braking. If you keep pushing with a constant force F, the rod speeds up until the braking force equals F: the terminal speed v<sub>T</sub> = FR/(B²L²), the same form as a falling object with drag.'],
 [('ε = <i class="bf">B</i><i>L</i><i class="cur">v</i>, &nbsp;<i class="cur">I</i> = <i class="bf">B</i><i>L</i><i class="cur">v</i>/<i>R</i>','motional EMF and current'),('<i class="frc">F</i><sub>mag</sub> = <i class="cur">I</i><i>L</i><i class="bf">B</i> = <i class="bf">B</i>²<i>L</i>²<i class="cur">v</i>/<i>R</i>','braking force, opposes v'),('<i class="cur">v</i><sub>T</sub> = <i>FR</i>/(<i class="bf">B</i>²<i>L</i>²)','terminal speed with a pushing force F')],
 lab_m,
 [('With no applied force, launch the rod at 4 m/s and watch it slow down.',[["#mo-F",0],["#mo-v0",4],["#mo-B",.5],["#mo-L",.4],["#mo-R",.5],["#mo-mass",.2],["click","#mo-go"]],'The current is largest at the start and decays as the rod slows: v and I fall together as e^(−t/τ). The kinetic energy all ends up as heat in the resistor.'),
  ('Double the magnetic field.',[["#mo-F",0],["#mo-v0",4],["#mo-B",1],["#mo-L",.4],["#mo-R",.5],["#mo-mass",.2],["click","#mo-go"]],'EMF doubles, current doubles, braking force quadruples (it goes as B²). The rod stops in a quarter of the distance.'),
  ('Push the rod with 1.0 N from rest.',[["#mo-F",1],["#mo-v0",0],["#mo-B",.5],["#mo-L",.4],["#mo-R",.5],["#mo-mass",.2],["click","#mo-go"]],'It speeds up, but the braking force grows with speed. When it equals the push, the acceleration is zero: a terminal speed v_T = FR/(B²L²) = 12.5 m/s.'),
  ('Increase the resistance and launch with no force.',[["#mo-F",0],["#mo-v0",4],["#mo-B",.5],["#mo-L",.4],["#mo-R",2],["#mo-mass",.2],["click","#mo-go"]],'A larger R means a smaller current, a weaker braking force, and a longer coast: the time constant mR/(B²L²) is 4 times longer.')],
 dict(prob='A 0.50 m conducting rod slides at 3.0 m/s along frictionless rails connected to a 2.0 Ω resistor, in a uniform 0.40 T field into the page. Find (a) the EMF, (b) the current and its direction, (c) the force needed to keep the rod moving at constant speed, and (d) the power. If the rod then has a mass of 0.10 kg and is released with no applied force, find its time constant.',svg=mo_svg,cap=mo_svg and 'The rod moves right through a field into the page. The current flows counterclockwise and the magnetic force on the rod points left.',
  steps=[('EMF.',m('ε = <i class="bf">B</i><i>L</i><i class="cur">v</i> = (0.40)(0.50)(3.0) = 0.60 V')+'.'),
   ('Current and direction.',m('<i class="cur">I</i> = ε/<i>R</i> = 0.60/2.0 = 0.30 A')+'. The flux into the page is growing, so the loop opposes it by making field out of the page: <b>counterclockwise</b>, which is upward through the rod.'),
   ('Force to move at constant speed.','The magnetic force on the rod is '+m('<i>F</i> = <i class="cur">I</i><i>L</i><i class="bf">B</i> = (0.30)(0.50)(0.40) = 0.060 N')+', directed left (opposing the motion), so you must push with 0.060 N to the right.'),
   ('Power.',m('<i>P</i> = <i class="cur">I</i>²<i>R</i> = (0.30)²(2.0) = 0.18 W')+'. Check: the push does work at the rate '+m('<i>Fv</i> = (0.060)(3.0) = 0.18 W')+' ✓: all of it becomes heat.'),
   ('Time constant when released.',m('τ = <i>mR</i>/(<i class="bf">B</i>²<i>L</i>²) = (0.10)(2.0)/((0.40)²(0.50)²) = 5.0 s')+'. The speed decays as '+m('<i>v</i> = 3.0<i>e</i><sup>−<i>t</i>/5.0</sup>')+', and the rod travels a total of '+m('<i>v</i><sub>0</sub>τ = 15 m')+' before stopping.')]),
 ['Putting the braking force the wrong way. It always opposes the rod\'s motion (Lenz\'s law).','Forgetting that the magnetic force depends on v, so the rod\'s acceleration is not constant.','Using B²L²v/R for the power: that is the force. Power is B²L²v²/R.','Saying that the rod loses energy to the magnetic field. The magnetic force does no net work on the charges; the energy goes to heat via the current.'],
 '''        <h2>Two ways to the same answer</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Magnetic force on charges:</b> in a rod moving with velocity v through B, charges feel <span class="m"><i>q</i><i class="cur">v</i> × <i class="bf">B</i></span> along the rod until an electric field builds up that balances it: <span class="m"><i class="ef">E</i> = <i class="cur">v</i><i class="bf">B</i></span>, so the voltage over length L is <span class="m"><i class="bf">B</i><i>L</i><i class="cur">v</i></span>.</li>
          <li><b>Faraday's law:</b> the area of the loop grows at rate <span class="m">d<i>A</i>/d<i>t</i> = <i>L</i><i class="cur">v</i></span>, so <span class="m">|ε| = <i class="bf">B</i>(d<i>A</i>/d<i>t</i>) = <i class="bf">B</i><i>L</i><i class="cur">v</i></span>. Same answer.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Energy and the equation of motion</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Newton's second law:</b> <span class="m"><i>m</i> d<i class="cur">v</i>/d<i>t</i> = <i>F</i> − <i class="bf">B</i>²<i>L</i>²<i class="cur">v</i>/<i>R</i></span>. This has the same form as drag, so <span class="m"><i class="cur">v</i> = <i class="cur">v</i><sub>T</sub> + (<i class="cur">v</i><sub>0</sub> − <i class="cur">v</i><sub>T</sub>)<i>e</i><sup>−<i>t</i>/τ</sup></span> with τ = <i>mR</i>/(<i class="bf">B</i>²<i>L</i>²).</li>
          <li><b>Without a force</b> (F = 0): <span class="m"><i class="cur">v</i> = <i class="cur">v</i><sub>0</sub><i>e</i><sup>−<i>t</i>/τ</sup></span> and the stopping distance is <span class="m"><i class="cur">v</i><sub>0</sub>τ</span>.</li>
          <li><b>Generator and eddy-current brake:</b> turning the rod into a rotating coil gives a generator; a metal sheet moving through a field makes swirling eddy currents that brake it.</li>
        </ul>''',
 dict(correct=1,explain='The EMF is BLv, so doubling v doubles the EMF and the current I = BLv/R. The braking force ILB = B²L²v/R is proportional to v, so it doubles too (it is proportional to B², not v²).',q='a rod slides on rails through a magnetic field. If you double the rod\'s speed, the magnetic braking force on it:',opts=['Stays the same','Doubles','Quadruples','Halves']))
